SUMMARY:Frank G. and Jean M. Chesley Lecture in Astronomy with Dr. Steven 
 Finkelstein (UT Austin)
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
LOCATION:Olin 149
DESCRIPTION:Dr. Steven Finkelstein from the University of Texas\, Austin\,
  will deliver this year's Chesley Lecture in Astronomy.\n\n&nbsp\;\n\n<str
 ong>Our Cosmic Origins - Early Galaxies and Black Holes</strong>\n\nWhen d
 id the first stars\, galaxies and black holes form in the universe? The Hu
 bble Space Telescope\, for all its amazing discoveries\, could not observe
  the earliest phases of structure formation\, in the first 500 million yea
 rs after the Big Bang. The launch of the James Webb Space Telescope (JWST)
  in 2021 has revolutionized this field\, providing the first observation o
 f the earliest phases of structure formation in the universe. From the fir
 st images to drop\, astronomers were stunned by what we saw. We found an e
 arly universe filled with more galaxies than expected\, a completely unexp
 ected population of “little red dots”\, and abundant growing black hol
 es. I will tell the story of these earliest observations\, explain why ast
 ronomers were so stunned\, and highlight our current attempts to reconcile
  our theories of galaxy and black hole formation with these revolutionary 
 new observations.\n\nView this event on the Carleton College website:\nhtt
 ps://www.carleton.edu/_c/uucq
ORGANIZER;CN="Physics And Astronomy Events":mailto:h1f12ld5t0uhukoqvsdt76p
 rj0@group.calendar.google.com
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260505T165513
END:VEVENT
