SUMMARY:Japanese Garden 50th Anniversary Symposium and Celebration
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
LOCATION:Symposium in Weitz Cinema | Reception at Japanese Garden
DESCRIPTION:Carleton College is celebrating the 50th Anniversary of Carlet
 on’s <em>Jōryō-en</em>\, “The Garden of Quiet Listening.” This hal
 f-day event features a morning symposium examining the educational potenti
 al and artistic history of Japanese gardens\, at Carleton and beyond. A ce
 lebratory tented reception with food and refreshments will follow in the y
 ard adjacent to the garden.  The event is free and open to the public. We
  hope you will join us to commemorate this special occasion!\n\nProgram Sc
 hedule:\n\n9:00-9:15 AM – Welcome Remarks\n\n9:15-9:45 AM - Keynote spea
 ker: <strong>Professor Kendall Brown</strong>\, Professor of Art History\,
  California State University\, Long Beach\, and author of <em>Quiet Beauty
 : The Japanese Gardens of North America</em>\, “Higher Education: The Po
 tential of Japanese Gardens for American Colleges”\n\n9:45-10:15 AM - <s
 trong>John Powell</strong>\, Zōen\, LLC\, Japanese Garden Designer and co
 nsultant to <em>Jōryō-en</em>\, “Tending the Vision:  The Gardener's 
 Role in the High Quality Japanese Garden"\n\n10:15 – 10:30 AM – Q and 
 A with the speakers\n\n10:30-10:45 AM – Coffee break\n\n10:45 – 11:15 
 AM - <strong>Anne Godfrey ’97</strong>\, Associate Professor of Landscap
 e Architecture\, SUNY College of Environmental Science and Forestry\, “A
 uthenticity Lessons”. Godfrey's work in <em>Jōryō-en</em> from 1997-19
 99\, under the tutelage of Mary Bigelow\, David Slawson and Bardwell Smith
 \, led her to a career in landscape architecture and photography.\n\n11:15
  -11:30 AM - <strong>Joanne Paul</strong>\, Northfield volunteer at <em>J
 ōryō-en</em>\, “Living\, Loving\, Working: The Garden from a Volunteer
 ’s Perspective”\n\n11:30-11:45 AM – Q and A with the speakers\n\n11:
 45 AM – 12:15 PM - <strong>Kathleen Ryor</strong>\, Tanaka Memorial Prof
 essor of International Understanding and Art History\, Carleton College\, 
 “<em>Jōryō-en</em> and Carleton: Recent Activities\, Future Directions
 ”\n\n<strong>12:30 – 2:00 PM – Reception at<em> Jōryō-en</em>.</st
 rong>  There will be staff to guide attendees to the garden area and assi
 st with parking directions.\n\n<strong>Location &amp\; Parking:</strong>\n
 <ul>\n 	<li>Symposium: Weitz Cinema Theater\, Weitz Center for Creativity<
 /li>\n 	<li>Reception: <em>Jōryō-en</em>\, "The Garden of Quiet Listenin
 g"</li>\n 	<li>Directions &amp\; Parking: Guest parking is available in ad
 jacent campus lots and along nearby public streets. Find directions and ac
 cessible parking areas on the<a href="https://www.carleton.edu/map/"> Carl
 eton Campus Map</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<a href="https://www.carleton.edu/japane
 se-garden/">Read more on Carleton's Japanese Garden here.</a>\n\nEvent Det
 ails:\nhttps://www.carleton.edu/japanese-garden/\n\nView this event on the
  Carleton College website:\nhttps://www.carleton.edu/_c/uuhL
ORGANIZER;CN="Asian Studies":mailto:qf2ecjm6il0qtd5cvsvp849qd0@group.calen
 dar.google.com
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260413T202004
END:VEVENT
