DTEND:20260928T163000
SUMMARY:Biology Seminar: Mindy Price\, Assistant Professor of Environmenta
 l Studies\, Macalester College\, “Spectacles of the Arctic Agricultural 
 Frontier”
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
LOCATION:Olin 141
DESCRIPTION:Climate change is reshaping our world\, not only through ongoi
 ng crises\, but through anticipated possibilities. In the rapidly warming 
 Arctic\, political\, economic\, and environmental futures are increasingly
  imagined and contested. Climate crises endanger Arctic ecosystems and com
 munities\, while melting ice and warming temperatures are increasing globa
 l speculation for new resource extraction\, transportation\, and territori
 al boundary-making. In this talk\, I will unpack scholarly visions of the 
 Arctic as a new agricultural frontier. Drawing on seven years of research 
 in the Northwest Territories\, Canada\, I will discuss how scientists use 
 climate projections and crop suitability models to depict the Arctic as a 
 future breadbasket\, and how this vision contrasts sharply with the experi
 ences of local Indigenous growers and small-scale commercial producers. Dr
 awing on political ecology’s concept of <i>socionatures </i>– that s
 ocial and environmental systems are inseparable – I show how the Arctic 
 is changing\, and people are changing too\, in sometimes contentious and u
 nexpected ways.\n\nView this event on the Carleton College website:\nhttps
 ://www.carleton.edu/_c/ut5f
ORGANIZER;CN="Biology":mailto:ud5qr7lq36rlnaka8qqqj7h6j8@group.calendar.go
 ogle.com
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260817T140539
END:VEVENT
