BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:kbh598bkv318h5q0209niaeauk
SEQUENCE:0
DTSTAMP:20260924T045925
DTSTART:20261002T153000
DTEND:20261002T163000
SUMMARY:Chemistry Department Seminar: Clay Tydings '21
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
LOCATION:Anderson 329
DESCRIPTION:<strong>Computational modeling and discovery of ribosomally sy
 nthesized and post-translationally modified peptides.</strong>\n\n<a href=
 "https://www.carleton.edu/chemistry/overview/events/seminars/speakers/clay
 -tydings-21/">Official/Unofficial Bio</a>\n\nAbstract: <span style="font-w
 eight: 400">Ribosomally synthesized and post-translationally modified pept
 ides (RiPPs) are a class of naturally occurring peptides with interesting 
 antibiotic\, antifungal and antitumor properties. RiPPs usually have a lea
 der peptide that serves as an enzyme recognition tag and a core peptide th
 at is enzymatically modified and becomes the mature\, active natural produ
 ct. For example\, the lanthipeptide RiPP class has characteristic thioethe
 r sidechain conjugations that are proteolytically and conformationally sta
 bilizing. A prior limitation of molecular design software such as the Rose
 tta macromolecular modeling software was the inability to model lanthipept
 ides. To enhance computational modeling and design of lanthipeptides\, I i
 mplemented support for lanthipeptides in Rosetta and used Rosetta to ident
 ify lanthipeptide ensembles that were lower in Rosetta energy\, more confo
 rmationally flexible\, and have similar agreement to experimental NMR data
  when compared to the PDB deposited ensembles. Additionally\, I developed 
 an AI-based method called leader peptide prediction (LPPred) for RiPP disc
 overy. A major objective of LPPred was to develop a model that generalizes
  across RiPP classes and overcomes the limitations of existing class speci
 fic genome mining tools such as RODEO. LPPred inputs a RiPP modifying enzy
 mes’ RiPP recognition element (RRE) and predicts the native RiPP leader 
 peptide recognized by the input RRE. LPPred demonstrates the potential to 
 accelerate genome mining for unknown RiPP classes. Overall\, this work dev
 elops new methods for computational modeling of lanthipeptides and RiPP di
 scovery.</span>\n\n&nbsp\;\n\n&nbsp\;\n\nView this event on the Carleton C
 ollege website:\nhttps://www.carleton.edu/_c/uujA
ORGANIZER;CN="Chemistry":mailto:alstmdjd7ad30s8q6rmu1tc548@group.calendar.
 google.com
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260911T190656
END:VEVENT
