SUMMARY:Biology Seminar: Rebecca Clements\, Assistant Professor of Biology
 \, Kenyon College\, "Neonatal red blood cells and their unexpected role in
  antiviral immunity"
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
LOCATION:Olin 141
DESCRIPTION:Red blood cells are typically thought of as highly specialized
  cells whose primary role is to transport oxygen throughout the body. Thei
 r lack of a nucleus and other organelles limits their capacity for many of
  the functions performed by other cells. However\, nucleated red blood cel
 l precursors circulate in human newborns and retain a remarkable range of 
 cellular functions. Our research has shown that\, in addition to their can
 onical role in oxygen transport\, these cells express pattern recognition 
 receptors that sense viral RNA and DNA and initiate antiviral immune respo
 nses. Research in the Clements Lab explores how neonatal red blood cells d
 etect and respond to viral infection and the consequences of these respons
 es\, revealing an unexpected connection between red blood cells and innate
  immunity.\n\nView this event on the Carleton College website:\nhttps://ww
 w.carleton.edu/_c/ut77
ORGANIZER;CN="Biology":mailto:ud5qr7lq36rlnaka8qqqj7h6j8@group.calendar.go
 ogle.com
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260820T144331
END:VEVENT
