DTSTART:20261009T105000
DTEND:20261009T115000
SUMMARY:Convocation with Valeria Luiselli
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
LOCATION:Skinner Memorial Chapel
DESCRIPTION:Valeria Luiselli was born in Mexico City and grew up in South 
 Korea\, South Africa and India. An acclaimed writer of both fiction and no
 nfiction\, she is the author of the essays <a>Tell Me How It Ends and <
 /a><a>Sidewalks</a><a>\; the sonic essay </a><a>Echoes From the Borderlan
 ds\;</a><a> </a>and the novels <a>Faces in the Crowd</a>\, <a>The Story
  of My Teeth</a>\, <a>Lost Children Archive</a><a>\, </a><a>and </a><a>
 Beginning Middle End.</a>\n\nShe is the recipient of a 2019 MacArthur Fell
 owship and the winner of DUBLIN Literary Award\, two Los Angeles Times Boo
 k Prizes\, The Carnegie Medal\, an American Book Award\,  and has been no
 minated for the National Book Critics Circle Award\, the Kirkus Prize\, an
 d the Booker Prize. She has been the recipient of a Bearing Witness Fellow
 ship from the Art for Justice Fund\, a Guggenheim Fellowship and the Vilce
 k Prize. Her work has appeared in The New Yorker\, The New York Times\,
  Granta\, and McSweeney's\, among other publications\, and has been tran
 slated into more than thirty languages. Her ongoing project in sound-scape
 s and oral histories\, Echoes From the Borderlands\, has been featured at
  the <a>DIA Chelsea</a> and the <a>Harvard Art Lab\, among other places
 .</a>\n\nView this event on the Carleton College website:\nhttps://www.car
 leton.edu/_c/ut5r
ORGANIZER;CN="Convocations":mailto:g8h62604doarbi9ihcipmnhd04@group.calend
 ar.google.com
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260806T162200
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:317v93mcdd16g3lo7jobo6ecf0
SEQUENCE:0
DTSTAMP:20260924T045925
DTSTART:20261009T121500
DTEND:20261009T131500
SUMMARY:Minneapolis Downtown Luncheon with Steve Poskanzer P'15
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
LOCATION:Offices of Fredrikson &amp\; Byron\n60 South 6th St\, Suite 1500\
 nMinneapolis\, MN 55402
DESCRIPTION:<h2 style="text-align: center">Twin Cities Carls are\ninvited 
 to the</h2>\n<h3 style="text-align: center">Minneapolis Downtown Luncheon 
 with\nSteve Poskanzer P'15</h3>\n<h3 style="text-align: center">"Speak Up 
 or Shut Up?<img class="alignright" src="https://cdn.carleton.edu/uploads/s
 ites/736/2026/09/unnamed.jpg" alt="" width="279" height="279" data-name="i
 mage" /> When Should Colleges Take Institutional Stances on Public Issues"
 </h3>\n<strong><u>Date</u></strong><u></u><b></b>\nFriday\, October 9\,  
 2026\n\n<strong><u>Time\n</u></strong><u></u><b></b>12:15-1:15 p.m.\n\n<st
 rong><u>Location\n</u></strong>Offices of Fredrikson &amp\; Byron\n<a href
 ="https://www.google.com/maps/place/Fredrikson+%26+Byron\,+P.A./@44.978311
 1\,-93.2728207\,830m/data=!3m3!1e3!4b1!5s0x52b3329a09681125:0x29614a50d953
 d315!4m6!3m5!1s0x52b3329a2a907aeb:0xbe5a9eb9ccf4c3f2!8m2!3d44.9783073!4d-9
 3.2702458!16s%2Fg%2F1tdjqstr!5m1!1e1?entry=tts&amp\;g_ep=EgoyMDI2MDgyNi4wI
 Pu8ASoASAFQAw%3D%3D&amp\;skid=2e85dab8-1c52-40eb-a17d-c9742a86a2e5">60 Sou
 th 6th St\, Suite 1500</a>\nMinneapolis\, MN 55402\n\n<strong><u>Cost\n</u
 ></strong>Cost: $15 per person (includes lunch). It will not be possible t
 o accommodate walk-ins.\n\n<strong><u>Register\n</u></strong>Please <a hre
 f="https://www.alumniq.carleton.edu/go/dwntwnlunch26">register</a> by Sund
 ay\, October 4.\n\n&nbsp\;\n\nUniversities and colleges face increasing pr
 essure to speak out on today's most contentious political\, moral\, and so
 cial issues. But when should an institution of higher learning take a stan
 d—and when is silence the wiser choice? In <em>The University's Voice</
 em>\, <strong>Steve Poskanzer P'15</strong> offers a timely and practica
 l guide for institutional leaders grappling with these high-stakes questio
 ns.\n\nEconomics Program in Cambridge (formerly Economics and European Stu
 dies) celebrated its 43rd anniversary in 2026. Since 1983\, this program h
 as engaged and challenged Carleton students to broaden and reimagine their
  world view through living and learning at one of the world’s great univ
 ersities and exploring one of the world’s great capitals.\n\nWith decade
 s of experience as a college president\, Poskanzer examines the growing de
 mands for universities to take official positions on matters ranging from 
 climate change to global conflicts\, to current debates over public policy
 . He argues that universities must be faithful to and focused upon their c
 ore mission—advancing knowledge and learning—while carefully navigatin
 g the fragile "social compact" between academia and broader society. At th
 e heart of his argument is the "General Rule of Reticence\," a principle t
 hat universities should be circumspect about adopting institutional positi
 ons unless doing so is essential to the pursuit of their mission\, includi
 ng safeguarding academic freedom. But reticence does not mean cowed silenc
 e. Poskanzer outlines clear\, actionable criteria for when universities ca
 n and even must speak out: when they possess unique educational expertise 
 to offer\; when speech will ensure a robust intellectual environment for f
 aculty and students\; when their mission is at issue\; or when their very 
 existence is at risk.\n\n<strong><u>Questions?\n</u></strong>Contact Alumn
 i Relations via <a href="alumni-office@carleton.edu"><strong>email</strong
 ></a> or 800-729-2586.\n\nView this event on the Carleton College website:
 \nhttps://www.carleton.edu/_c/uufd
ORGANIZER;CN="Carleton’s Regional Carls Program":mailto:r7km2dafnqvrponn
 mp387i2vgg@group.calendar.google.com
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260901T221346
END:VEVENT
