DTSTART:20261012T153000
DTEND:20261012T163000
SUMMARY:Jaci Saunders\, Assistant Professor of Marine Sciences\, Universit
 y of Georgia\, "From Peptides to Plankton: Proteomic Insights into Marine 
 Microbial Communities"
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
LOCATION:Olin 141
DESCRIPTION:Microbial proteins are the engines of major biogeochemical cyc
 les\, driving biochemical conversions and habitability in the surface ocea
 n. Microbial metaproteomics enables a high-throughput glimpse into these m
 icrobial engines\, including the interactions between microbial community 
 members and their surrounding environments. The ProteOMZ research expediti
 on surveyed the proteomes of the free-living microbial communities (0.2 to
  3 µm) along a 5\,000-km-long\, 1-km-deep transect in the central Pacific
  Ocean. Eighty-five percent of total protein abundance was of bacterial or
 igin\, with Archaea contributing 1.6%. Over 2\,000 functional KEGG Ontolog
 y (KO) groups were identified\, yet only 25 KO groups contributed over hal
 f of the protein abundance\, simultaneously indicating abundant key functi
 ons and a long tail of diverse functions. Community protein expression var
 ied along horizontal and vertical scales\, aligning with key environmental
  features of major oceanic regimes. Unsupervised clustering analyses revea
 led syntrophic dynamics between community members along the transect. High
 -throughput proteomic surveys can provide key insights into the integral m
 icrobial communities of the surface ocean and the key elemental cycles the
 y mediate.\n\nEvent Details:\nhttps://www.jaclynsaunders.com/\n\nView this
  event on the Carleton College website:\nhttps://www.carleton.edu/_c/uueJ
ORGANIZER;CN="Biology":mailto:ud5qr7lq36rlnaka8qqqj7h6j8@group.calendar.go
 ogle.com
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260915T143728
END:VEVENT
