DTSTART:20261013T171500
DTEND:20261013T184500
SUMMARY:Fall 2026 Lefler Lecture: Dr. Rashauna Johnson  Bittersweet Homes:
  Placemaking in a Parish Haunted by History
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
LOCATION:Leighton Hall 305
DESCRIPTION:<strong>Bittersweet Homes: Placemaking in a Parish Haunted by 
 History</strong>\n\nThis talk uses archival research and family memories t
 o illuminate the lives and times of select figures who died and\, critical
 ly\, also lived in the long arc of King Cotton. Drawing on select moments 
 in the history of the author's maternal grandmother's birthplace\, Louisia
 na's Feliciana Parishes\, it meditates on the deeply contingent experience
 s of 19th-Century "development" and "progress" and the ways those divergen
 t experiences - and the memories to which they gave rise - haunt us all.\n
 \nRashauna Johnson is a historian of the 19th-century African diaspora\, w
 ith an emphasis on slavery and emancipation in the US South and Atlantic W
 orld. She is especially interested in the limits and possibilities of arch
 ival histories of enslaved and freed people and the worlds in which they l
 abored and lived. Johnson teaches courses on race\, slavery\, and nation\;
  methodologies of slavery studies\; and the 19th-century US.\n\nJohnson is
  the author of <a href="https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/slaverys-met
 ropolis/40469AB30465025FFBE1C4E959A55083" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><
 em>Slavery’s Metropolis: Unfree Labor in New Orleans during the Age of R
 evolutions</em></a> (Cambridge UP\, 2016\; paperback 2018)\, which was aw
 arded the 2016 Williams Prize for the best book in Louisiana history and t
 he 2018 H. L. Mitchell Award by the Southern Historical Association for th
 e best book on the southern working class. <em>Slavery's Metropolis </em
 >was also named a finalist for the 2016 Berkshire Conference of Women's Hi
 storians Book Prize\, honorable mention for the Urban History Association'
 s Kenneth Jackson Award\, and a finalist for the 2017 Frederick Douglass B
 ook Prize. Her second book\, <a href="https://www.cambridge.org/us/unive
 rsitypress/subjects/history/african-american-histor/sweet-home-feliciana-f
 amily-slavery-and-hauntings-history?format=HB&amp\;isbn=9781009668323"><em
 >Sweet Home Feliciana: Family\, Slavery\, and the Hauntings of History</em
 ></a> (Cambridge University Press\, 2026)\, uses her grandmother’s birt
 hplace in rural Louisiana as a prism to illuminate foundational\, if fraug
 ht\, aspects of US history including colonialism\, slavery\, war\, citizen
 ship and unfinished freedom. That project has been supported by the Hutch
 ins Center for African &amp\; African American Research at Harvard Univers
 ity\, the Mellon Scholars Post-Doctoral Fellowship in African American Hi
 story at the Library Company of Philadelphia\, and The Gilder Lehrman Inst
 itute of American History. She is a co-editor of the <em>Cambridge Histo
 ry of the African Diaspora\, Volume Two: Slave Trades\, Dispersals\, and P
 rocesses of Commodification </em>(forthcoming 2027).\n\nView this event o
 n the Carleton College website:\nhttps://www.carleton.edu/_c/uufg
ORGANIZER;CN="History":mailto:13j4s2le5qmt8ia3lrh1m7345o@group.calendar.go
 ogle.com
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260511T212444
END:VEVENT
