SUMMARY:The Frank G. and Jean M. Chesley Lectureship in Environmental Stud
 ies by Dr. Jessica Hernandez: Fresh Roots\, Rising Waters: Indigenous Scie
 nce as the Blueprint for Climate Justice
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
LOCATION:Olin 149
DESCRIPTION:<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-2090f57f eleme
 ntor-widget__width-initial elementor-widget elementor-widget-theme-post-co
 ntent" data-id="2090f57f" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" 
 data-widget_type="theme-post-content.default">\n<div class="elementor-widg
 et-container">\n<div dir="ltr">For generations\, Indigenous science has be
 en dismissed as folklore rather than recognized as rigorous\, land-based k
 nowledge accumulated through direct relationship with ecosystems. Dr. Jess
 ica Hernandez (Maya Ch'orti' &amp\; Binnizá) draws on her work as founder
  of Earth Daughters and author of <em>Fresh Banana Leaves</em> and <em>
 Growing Papaya Trees</em> to reframe the climate conversation: colonialism
  didn't just displace Indigenous peoples from their lands\, it displaced t
 heir knowledge systems from the institutions now scrambling to solve the c
 risis those systems could have prevented. This keynote traces the throughl
 ine from ecological healing to climate displacement\, and makes the case t
 hat climate justice is inseparable from Indigenous sovereignty\; over land
 \, over research\, and over the frameworks we use to define what counts as
  science.</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n\nView this event on the Carleton College
  website:\nhttps://www.carleton.edu/_c/ut2U
ORGANIZER;CN="Environmental Studies":mailto:6f79fpli726ta90o93g2pcmhfo@gro
 up.calendar.google.com
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260616T161719
END:VEVENT
