SUMMARY:Arboretum Centennial Celebration
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
LOCATION:<a href="https://www.carleton.edu/map/?id=2017#!ct/61574\,61240\,
 61237\,61587\,61588\,61586\,63776\,61789\,61790\,61607\,72489\,72490\,7249
 1\,72492\,72493\,72465\,93830?fh/?m/1088405?s/boardman%2520memorial?mc/44.
 464392906191875\,-93.1475165557942?z/17.064683478265685?lvl/0?share">Board
 man Memorial</a>
DESCRIPTION:For a century\, the Cowling Arboretum has played an integral r
 ole in education\, conservation\, and recreation for Carleton and the Nort
 hfield community. We invite you to join us in celebrating this milestone a
 nd College’s role in developing the state’s first arboretum — a dive
 rse landscape that is central to our identity and a beloved resource for a
 ll.\n\nJoin us for this joyous celebration\, including:\n<ul>\n 	<li>Comme
 morative tree planting to honor the legacy of President Donald Cowling</li
 >\n 	<li>Celebration of Professor Harvey E. Stork and Grounds Superintende
 nt D. Blake Stewart who originally proposed and developed the Arboretum</l
 i>\n 	<li>Formal remarks from College and community leaders</li>\n 	<li>Pe
 rformance by the Carleton Choir and Opening Song by Reuben Kitto Stately 
 (Red Lake Ojibwe and Santee Dakota)</li>\n 	<li><span style="font-weight: 
 400">Reception following the formal program for</span>\n<ul>\n 	<li>Light 
 refreshments</li>\n 	<li>Visits with faculty and students using the Arb fo
 r research and academics</li>\n 	<li>Guided walking tours\, led by staff a
 nd student naturalists</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<b>Event Location &amp\;
  Parking details:</b>\n\nThis event will take place at the <a href="https:
 //www.carleton.edu/map/?id=2017#!ct/61574\,61240\,61237\,61587\,61588\,615
 86\,63776\,61789\,61790\,61607\,72489\,72490\,72491\,72492\,72493\,72465\,
 93830?fh/?m/1088405?s/boardman%2520memorial?mc/44.464392906191875\,-93.147
 5165557942?z/17.064683478265685?lvl/0?share">Boardman Memorial</a>\, behin
 d the Rec Center and on the <span style="font-weight: 400">edge of the Upp
