DTEND:20261029T180000
SUMMARY:Paying for the Public Life: Charles W. Moore and the Public Realm
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
LOCATION:Boliou 104
DESCRIPTION:<em>This talk is part of the Edwin L. Weisl Jr. Lecture Series
  sponsored by the Robert Lehman Foundation</em>\n\nArchitectural historian
  and activist\, Patricia Morton will give a talk that focuses on writings 
 and buildings by Charles W. Moore\, a prominent Postmodern architect\, who
  asserted that “you have to pay for the public life” in a famous 1965 
 essay. Looking at several of Moore’s projects from the 1960s and 1970s\,
  the talk will investigate what Moore meant by “paying” for the public
  realm\, his advocacy for Disneyland as a public space\, and the ways he u
 nderstood “the public” in various contexts\, including a public housin
 g project and student housing at a public university\n\n<hr />\n\nPatricia
  A. Morton is Associate Professor in the Media and Cultural Studies Depart
 ment at the University of California\, Riverside.\n\nShe is author of <em
 >Hybrid Modernities: Architecture and Representation at the 1931 Internati
 onal Colonial Exposition in Paris </em>(MIT Press). Her current project\,
  <em>Paying for the Public Lif</em>e\, focuses on work by architect Charl
 es W. Moore and changes to the public realm in the 1960s.\n\nView this eve
 nt on the Carleton College website:\nhttps://www.carleton.edu/_c/uueA
ORGANIZER;CN="Art & Art History":mailto:8ckv8vtit6m6sj5p1qnvn5qq14@group.c
 alendar.google.com
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260824T170146
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:gj38q0vgsmndb2si9eesdlsujk
SEQUENCE:0
DTSTAMP:20260924T045925
DTSTART:20261029T171500
DTEND:20261029T181500
SUMMARY:Greece at a Crossroads: History\, Landscape\, and Material Culture
  Info Session
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
LOCATION:Leighton 236
DESCRIPTION:Greece is one of the most fascinating and beautiful places in 
 the world. With a base in Athens and trips to some of the most storied sit
 es in all of Europe—Delphi\, Olympia\, the Greek isles—this program co
 mbines a long-term view of the Greek world with courses and practical trai
 ning in a variety of academic disciplines. In addition to a core course on
  the history\, landscape\, and material culture of Greece\, students will 
 choose from several elective courses concerning the contemporary world: le
 gacies of empire\, human mobility and migration\, and the politics and eco
 nomy of this cultural crossroads between Europe\, Africa\, and Asia.\n\nEv
 ent Details:\nhttps://www.carleton.edu/ocs/greece/\n\nView this event on t
 he Carleton College website:\nhttps://www.carleton.edu/_c/uujd
ORGANIZER;CN="Off Campus Studies":mailto:4iqiub2cjhnjgpqu2joiv600f8@group.
 calendar.google.com
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260922T215139
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:021o3l5461rtt6uu4cgkaftrl4
SEQUENCE:0
DTSTAMP:20260924T045925
DTSTART:20261029T193000
DTEND:20261029T203000
SUMMARY:Sport and Globalization in London &amp\; Seville Info Session
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
LOCATION:Leighton 236
DESCRIPTION:What role does sport play in a society? Given their deep sport
 ing history and current success on the world stage\, London (England) and 
 Seville (Spain) provide rich and unique opportunities to investigate how s
 port and society intersect\, as well as how globalization impacts each. Th
 e program will critically examine sport and sporting culture in both count
 ries.\n\nEvent Details:\nhttps://www.carleton.edu/ocs/london-seville\n\nVi
 ew this event on the Carleton College website:\nhttps://www.carleton.edu/_
 c/ut8n
ORGANIZER;CN="Off Campus Studies":mailto:4iqiub2cjhnjgpqu2joiv600f8@group.
 calendar.google.com
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260728T204050
END:VEVENT
