BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:u38e88i3lb3l5tldgcnvgodjks
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20260924T045925
DTSTART:20261105T160000
DTEND:20261105T170000
SUMMARY:Reading *Trust* with Hernán Díaz
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
LOCATION:TBD
DESCRIPTION:<p style="font-weight: 400"><a href="https://www.hernandiaz.ne
 t/">Hernán Díaz</a>\, one of the most distinctive voices in contemporary
  American fiction\, will visit Carleton on November 4–5 as the special g
 uest of the Light Lectureship.</p>\n<p style="font-weight: 400">In anticip
 ation of his visit\, we invite faculty and staff to join a reading group d
 evoted to his Pulitzer Prize-winning novel <a href="https://www.hernandiaz
 .net/trust-novel"><em>Trust</em></a>\, culminating in a conversation with 
 Diaz himself.</p>\n<p style="font-weight: 400">Díaz’s first novel\, <e
 m>In the Distance</em>\, was a finalist for the Pulitzer Prize\; his secon
 d\, <em>Trust</em>\, won the 2023 Pulitzer Prize for Fiction.</p>\n<p sty
 le="font-weight: 400">Across these two remarkably different novels\, Díaz
  returns to some of the foundational myths of American culture — the fro
 ntier\, the self-made individual\, money\, power\, and the creation of eno
 rmous wealth — while radically reinventing the literary forms through wh
 ich those myths have traditionally been told. <em>In the Distance</em> re
 imagines the western from the perspective of a lost immigrant moving again
 st the great westward migration\; <em>Trust</em> takes apart the novel o
 f wealth and finance by telling the same story through four competing narr
 atives. In both\, Diaz inhabits recognizable American genres only to estra
 nge them from within\, asking who gets to tell the stories through which A
 merica imagines itself.</p>\n<p style="font-weight: 400">The reading group
  will meet once to discuss <em>Trust </em>(date/time TBD). On November 4\
 , Díaz will give a talk about his fiction and how he situates his work wi
 thin American literature. On November 5\, members of the group will have t
 he opportunity to discuss <em>Trust</em> with Díaz himself — a rare ch
 ance to continue our reading in conversation with the author.</p>\n<p styl
 e="font-weight: 400">The reading group is sponsored by the Department of S
 panish in collaboration with the Humanities Center. Copies of <em>Trust</e
 m> will be available for all participants interested in joining the group
 . If you would like to participate and receive a copy of the novel\, pleas
 e contact Jorge Brioso (<a href="mailto:jbrioso@carleton.edu">jbrioso@carl
 eton.edu</a>) and Dan Groll (<a href="mailto:dgroll@carleton.edu">dgroll@c
 arleton.edu</a>) by <strong>September 30</strong>.</p>\n\nView this event
  on the Carleton College website:\nhttps://www.carleton.edu/_c/uub8
ORGANIZER;CN="Humanities Center":mailto:k73j67gu67k2rvuvvoi4kh8ctc@group.c
 alendar.google.com
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260906T154519
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
